WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
A contractor schedules a facade inspection for late afternoon.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:09.720
The site is only 90 feet tall, but it sits between two buildings with trees along one side and a ridge upwind.

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:17.420
The aviation forecast is legal for Part 107, yet the handheld anemometer shows gusts rolling through the courtyard.

4
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.160
This is where weather knowledge becomes operational judgment rather than vocabulary.

5
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:36.680
Reliable aviation weather sources include the National Weather Service Aviation Weather Center, METARs for current observations, TAFs for forecasts at airports, graphical aviation forecasts, and official briefings when appropriate.

6
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:49.580
A METAR can show wind direction and speed, gusts, visibility, clouds, temperature, dew point, altimeter, and significant weather near the reporting station.

7
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.040
A TAF helps the pilot see expected changes over time.

8
00:00:53.140 --> 00:01:02.900
These products are not perfect descriptions of a small courtyard, roofline, or canyon, but they establish the larger air mass and trends that local observation must refine.

9
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:09.500
Wind matters because a small UAS has limited thrust, limited battery, and limited mass.

10
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.800
A headwind reduces groundspeed and can turn a short return leg into a long energy problem.

11
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:21.460
A tailwind outbound can be deceptive because the aircraft moves away quickly while the return requires more power.

12
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.440
Crosswinds complicate precise inspection tracks.

13
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.860
Gusts change stopping distance and position holding.

14
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:35.520
Mechanical turbulence forms downwind of buildings, trees, terrain, ridges, and confined spaces.

15
00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:42.340
A drone that hovers calmly over the launch point may work hard at roof height or lose precise control near the corner of a structure.

16
00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:46.700
Temperature, moisture, and elevation affect performance too.

17
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:53.120
Warm air and high-elevation conditions reduce air density, which can require more power for the same lift.

18
00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:58.820
Humidity can also reduce density enough to matter when the aircraft is already near its performance margin.

19
00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:05.640
Hot batteries may limit output or age faster; cold batteries may sag under load and trigger early warnings.

20
00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:11.660
Payload weight, accessory drag, propeller condition, and flight mode all change endurance.

21
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:18.320
A manufacturer wind limit or temperature limit is an aircraft limitation, not an invitation to fly at the edge.

22
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.080
Weather also affects the regulatory picture.

23
00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:31.900
Part 107 requires at least 3 statute miles of flight visibility from the control station and cloud clearance of at least 500 feet below and 2,000 feet horizontally.

24
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.960
Those numbers do not answer every mission question.

25
00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:47.140
Fog, haze, precipitation, low ceilings, glare, dark backgrounds, and blowing dust can reduce visual line of sight or make it hard to detect crewed traffic and obstacles.

26
00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:58.840
Thunderstorms and unstable air deserve conservative distance because gust fronts, lightning, precipitation, and rapid wind shifts can arrive faster than a short drone flight can recover.

27
00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:13.120
For the facade inspection, the remote pilot combines the METAR, TAF, local wind checks, observed tree movement, payload weight, route geometry, battery reserve, emergency landing choices, and manufacturer limits.

28
00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:24.820
The answer may be to delay until morning, inspect only the leeward side, relocate the launch point, reduce altitude, use a lighter payload, add a visual observer, or cancel.

29
00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:29.080
The takeaway is that legal weather minimums are not personal minimums.

30
00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:34.460
A safe go decision leaves enough performance to come home after the weather gets a little worse.
